%[text] # copytable
%[text] Copy MATLAB® tables to the clipboard for pasting into Excel® or any text-based application
%[text] 
%[text] ![](text:image:77b2)
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] ## Background
%[text] MATLAB provides functions for exporting tabular data to files, but there is currently no built-in way to copy the contents of a table directly to the system clipboard for use in other applications. Saving a table as a temporary file is time consuming when the goal is simply to move results from MATLAB into a spreadsheet, email, or document.
%[text] `copytable` fills this gap by allowing a MATLAB `table`, `timetable`, `eventtable`, and `uitable` to be copied directly to the clipboard in a single command. The resulting text can be pasted into Excel, emails, markdown files, spreadsheets, and other applications without creating temporary files.
%[text] Unlike MATLAB's [clipboard](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/clipboard.html) function, `copytable` accepts table-based data structures directly and automatically formats mixed-type tabular data for copying and pasting. 
%[text] This utility is particularly useful for:
%[text] - App Designer™ applications that display data in a `uitable`
%[text] - Sharing analysis results with colleagues
%[text] - Copying data to a markdown file using the | delimiter
%[text] - Quickly exporting datasets to Excel
%[text] - Exporting tabular data without creating temporary files \
%[text] ## Features
%[text] The input arguments to copytable are as follows:
%[text] 1. Required: The table to be copied
%[text] 2. Optional: The delimiter to be used. Input must be text scalar.
%[text] 3. Optional: Whether you want to include row headers. Input must be a logical scalar (i.e., `true` or `false`). \
%[text] Some additional capabilities are:
%[text] - Supports the MATLAB `table`, `timetable`, `eventtable`, and `uitable` objects
%[text] - Preserves full numeric precision
%[text] - Handles mixed data types within the same table
%[text] - Automatically generates column headers if included
%[text] - Requires no toolboxes beyond MATLAB \
%[text] Supported scalar data types include:
%[text] - Numeric (real and complex)
%[text] - Logical
%[text] - String
%[text] - Character vectors
%[text] - Datetime
%[text] - Duration
%[text] - Calendar duration
%[text] - Categorical
%[text] - Function handles
%[text] - Categorical
%[text] - Cell (see limitations) \
%[text] ## Typical Use
%[text] 1. Generate or display table data in MATLAB.
%[text] 2. Call `copytable` with the table as the input argument with optional arguments for the delimiter type and whether to include headers.
%[text] 3. Switch to Excel.
%[text] 4. Paste using **Ctrl+V**. \
%[text] ## Limitations
%[text] - Each table entry must be scalar. Non-scalar entries such as vectors, matrices, objects, or multidimensional arrays are not supported.
%[text] - Nested tables and other complex container objects such as cells are not supported.
%[text] - Cells containing nonscalar values cannot be copied.
%[text] - Row names are not copied.
%[text] - Variable units are not copied. \
%[text] For example:
unsupportedTable = table([1 2;3 4]);
try %[output:group:981e746c]
    copytable(unsupportedTable) %this should error as variables are not scalar
catch ME
    disp(ME.identifier) %[output:7a61f063]
end %[output:group:981e746c]
%[text] ## Toolbox Requirements
%[text] - MATLAB \
%[text] ## Compatibility
%[text] R2024a through R2026a. Untested in earlier versions.
%%
%[text] ## Example 1 - use with regular table
LastName = ["Sanchez";"Johnson";"Li";"Diaz";"Brown"];
Age = [38;43;38;40;49];
Smoker = logical([1;0;1;0;1]);
Height = [71;69;64;67;64];
Weight = [176;163;131;133;119];
T = table(LastName,Age,Smoker,Height,Weight);
disp(T) %[output:1c13475f]
copytable(T)
clipboard('paste') %or paste in Excel %[output:275b4156]
%%
%[text] ## Example 2 - use with uitable
dataCell = {'John', 25, 68.5; 'Sarah', 30, 72.0};
fig = uifigure;
uit = uitable(fig,'Data', dataCell, 'ColumnName', {'Name', 'Age', 'Weight'});
pause(1) %ensure figure and uitable load
copytable(uit)
delete(fig)
clipboard('paste') %or paste in Excel %[output:11d479dc]
%%
%[text] ## Example 3 - tables with different precision
defaultPi = pi*[1;2;3];
piInt = int8(defaultPi);
piHalf = half(defaultPi);
piSingle = single(defaultPi);
piDouble = double(defaultPi)*1;
piFormulas = {@(circ,diam)circ/diam; @(A,r) 2*A/r^2; @(V,r) V/r^3*9/4};
piTable = table(piInt,piHalf,piSingle,piDouble,piFormulas);
disp(piTable) %[output:06cdce1e]
copytable(piTable)
clipboard('paste') %or paste in Excel %[output:42face9e]
%%
%[text] ## Example 4 - use with timetable
MeasurementTime = datetime(["2015-12-18 08:03:05";"2015-12-18 10:03:17";"2015-12-18 12:03:13"]);
Temp = [37.3;39.1;42.3];
Pressure = [30.1;30.03;29.9];
WindSpeed = [13.4;6.5;7.3];
TT = timetable(MeasurementTime,Temp,Pressure,WindSpeed);
disp(TT) %[output:88ef4af0]
copytable(TT)
clipboard('paste') %or paste in Excel %[output:8bacfa32]
%%
%[text] ## Example 5 - use with eventtable
Time = datetime(2022,11,[3 5 10 14]);
labels = ["Hail","Rain","Snow","Rain"];
lengths = hours([1.2 36 18 20]);
ET = eventtable(Time,EventLabels=labels,EventLengths=lengths);
disp(ET) %[output:3afcbdad]
copytable(ET)
clipboard('paste') %or paste in Excel %[output:3d9b1f23]
%%
%[text] ## Example 6 - applying custom delimiters
%[text] Change the delimiter from the default "," to the tab delimiter "\\t".
Data = [-10-3i; 3+4i; 30];
rowTimes = (calmonths(1:3))';
tabTable = table(rowTimes, Data);
disp(tabTable) %[output:1c263f0a]
copytable(tabTable,'\t')
clipboard('paste') %[output:3c1bba6a]
%%
%[text] ## Example 7 - controlling whether table headers are included
%[text] The default value to copy the table header is set to `true`. Set this value to `false` to omit the header. 
Mixed = {42; "abc"; datetime(2025,1,1)};
Value = [1;2;3];
T = table(Mixed,Value);
disp(T) %[output:2ac82cd3]
copytable(T,"|",false)
clipboard('paste') %[output:67348a28]
%%
%[text] ## Performance
%[text] To get a baseline of performance, use `copytable` on a table containing 1 variable (column) with multiple rows. The runtime performance is proportional to the total number of copied values rather than number of columns or rows. It is faster if the data type being copied is not a cell because the function would need to check each individual cell for correctness which affects runtime performance.
numberOfRowsArray = ceil(logspace(1,6,10));
timingVector = zeros(size(numberOfRowsArray));
counter = 1; 
for ii = 1:numel(numberOfRowsArray)
    numberOfRows = numberOfRowsArray(ii);
    dataRow = string(rand(numberOfRows,1));
    tableRow = table(dataRow);
    timingVector(counter) = timeit(@() copytable(tableRow));
    counter = counter + 1;
end
plot(numberOfRowsArray,timingVector,'o-') %[output:47de669d]
xscale log %[output:47de669d]
xlabel('Number of Rows Copied') %[output:47de669d]
ylabel('Time (seconds)') %[output:47de669d]
title('copytable Performance') %[output:47de669d]

